# Semantic Line Breaks

Text is wrapped at the boundaries of meaning:
each sentence on its own line, and a long sentence split into its parts.
The technique is described at [sembr.org](https://sembr.org).

A diff then shows the changed sentence whole,
a review comment sticks to one thought,
and editing a phrase does not reflow a paragraph.

## Where to break

- Always after a full stop, a question mark and an exclamation mark.
- Where possible inside a long sentence, at the boundary of a part:
  after a colon or a semicolon,
  after a dash between parts,
  after a comma before a new clause or a new group of a list,
  before a parenthesis, and before a link.
- Never inside a phrase.
  A preposition or a conjunction moves to the next line with its word.

## Width

- About 100 characters is the target.
  A line may run longer to keep a phrase, a link or code whole.
- A line well over 120 characters is a reason to break at the nearest boundary or to shorten the sentence.

## Example

Before — wrapped by width, sentences cut in the middle:

```markdown
The tools start their own Chrome with a temporary profile and stop only that process. Never kill
Chrome by name: other instances may belong to the user, and a killed window loses their work.
```

After — wrapped by meaning:

```markdown
The tools start their own Chrome with a temporary profile and stop only that process.
Never kill Chrome by name:
other instances may belong to the user, and a killed window loses their work.
```

## Markdown

- The breaks are soft: the browser joins the lines of a paragraph.
- No trailing spaces: two of them make a visible line break.
- A continuation line is indented to the text of its item,
  two spaces after `- ` and three after `1. `.
  It does not start with `- `, `* `, `+ `, a number and a dot, `#`, `>` or `|`:
  Markdown would take it for a new list, heading, quotation or table.
- Table rows, headings, frontmatter, URLs and code blocks are not wrapped.
- A pull request description is Markdown, and the same rules apply.

## Russian and Chinese

- Russian follows the English rules.
- Chinese: a soft break turns into a space in the browser.
  Break only after full-width punctuation — 。，；：！？ — and never between two characters.
  One sentence per line.

## Comments in code

- The same habit: a sentence per line, and a long one split at a boundary.
- A comment at the end of a code line is one short phrase.
  A longer explanation is a block above the line.
- No trailing spaces.

## Check

`bun run check:docs` flags a line with two sentences, trailing spaces in Markdown,
and a Chinese break that a browser would show as a space.
It does not judge where a long sentence should split.
